          %h2#higher_dimensional_supergravity Higher dimensional supergravity
          %p
            In the contemporary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            literature this concept of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartan+connection"} Cartan connection
            may seem to play an orphaned role, at least if one compares the number of occurrences of the explicit term
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartan+connection"} Cartan connection
            in the physics literature over that of “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Levi-Civita+connection"}> Levi-Civita connection
            ”, and certainly when compared to the number of contemporary articles that speak of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geodesics"} geodesics
            without any concept of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection"} connection
            made explicit.
          %p
            However, this is partly an illusion. Examination of the literature shows that at least as soon as authors consider
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergeometry"} super
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartan+geometry"} Cartan geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermions"} fermions
            and of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry"} supersymmetry
            really natural.
          %p
            In fact, higher dimensional supergravity such as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+supergravity"} type II supergravity
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heterotic+supergravity"} heterotic supergravity
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/11-dimensional+supergravity"} 11-dimensional supergravity
            crucially includes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
            which are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+gauge+fields"} higher gauge fields
            – the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B2-field"} B2-field
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B6-field"} B6-field
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C3-field"} C3-field
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C6-field"} C6-field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/RR-field"} RR-field
            if one digs deeper.
          %p
            The only proposal in the physics literature for how to deal with such higher gauge higher supergravity theories
            %em geometrically
            is the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D%27Auria-Fre+formulation+of+supergravity"} D'Auria-Fre formulation of supergravity
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+Cartan+geometry"} higher Cartan geometry
          %p
            Here
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+L-%E2%88%9E+algebra"} super L-∞ algebra
            is the joint generalization of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Lie+algebra"} super Lie algebra
            and to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-%E2%88%9E+algebra"} L-∞ algebra
          .maruku-equation
            %math#mathml_a0136b00a59018dc879e6912a866dd696f262538_39.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
